{"term": "опустить", "lang": "ru", "script": "cyrl", "romanization": "opustit'", "category": "acts", "subcategory": "prison_caste_demotion", "concept": "prison_sexual_demotion", "is_novel_concept": true, "register": "subculture", "crude": 85, "technical": 5, "recognition_pct_current": 68, "recognition_pct_peak": 72, "peak_year": null, "regional": null, "definition": "In Russian prison culture, to forcibly sodomize an inmate as a ritual act of social degradation, permanently demoting the victim to the lowest-caste 'petukhi' status with all attendant prohibitions and ostracism.", "english_term": null, "english_translation_quality": "none", "notes": "The demotion is considered permanent and irreversible within prison culture; no subsequent act restores prior caste standing. Widely referenced in Russian crime literature, prison memoirs, and television drama."}
{"term": "опускание", "lang": "ru", "script": "cyrl", "romanization": "opuskaniye", "category": "acts", "subcategory": "prison_caste_demotion", "concept": "prison_sexual_demotion", "is_novel_concept": true, "register": "subculture", "crude": 85, "technical": 5, "recognition_pct_current": 65, "recognition_pct_peak": 70, "peak_year": null, "regional": null, "definition": "Nominalization of опустить; the ritual process of forcible sexual assault in Russian prison culture that permanently strips a victim of social standing and relegates them to the pariah caste.", "english_term": null, "english_translation_quality": "none", "notes": "Used both for the discrete act and the broader social process of caste demotion. Appears in criminological and sociological literature on Russian prison subculture."}
{"term": "гнус", "lang": "ru", "script": "cyrl", "romanization": "gnus", "category": "honor_shame_economy", "subcategory": "prison_pariah_caste", "concept": "prison_pariah_caste_label", "is_novel_concept": true, "register": "subculture", "crude": 75, "technical": 5, "recognition_pct_current": 38, "recognition_pct_peak": 45, "peak_year": null, "regional": null, "definition": "Prison slang label for an inmate at the very bottom of the caste hierarchy — below ordinary петухи in some regional prison systems — connoting extreme degradation and untouchable status.", "english_term": null, "english_translation_quality": "none", "notes": "Primary civilian meaning is 'gnat' or 'biting midge,' evoking pestilential worthlessness. Regional variation exists: in some institutions synonymous with петух, in others designates a distinct sub-caste below it."}
{"term": "петушня", "lang": "ru", "script": "cyrl", "romanization": "petushnya", "category": "honor_shame_economy", "subcategory": "prison_pariah_caste", "concept": "prison_pariah_caste_collective", "is_novel_concept": true, "register": "subculture", "crude": 80, "technical": 5, "recognition_pct_current": 52, "recognition_pct_peak": 58, "peak_year": null, "regional": null, "definition": "Collective noun for the 'petukhi' caste — the sexually degraded inmates at the bottom of Russian prison hierarchy; also used loosely for any gathering of despised or worthless people.", "english_term": null, "english_translation_quality": "none", "notes": "Formed from петух (rooster) + collective suffix -ня. 'Rooster' in Russian prison slang designates a male who has been anally penetrated, hence the lowest-status caste; the collective form denotes them as a group."}
{"term": "петушатник", "lang": "ru", "script": "cyrl", "romanization": "petushatnik", "category": "honor_shame_economy", "subcategory": "prison_pariah_caste", "concept": "prison_pariah_caste_space", "is_novel_concept": true, "register": "subculture", "crude": 75, "technical": 5, "recognition_pct_current": 48, "recognition_pct_peak": 55, "peak_year": null, "regional": null, "definition": "The designated cell or section of a prison block reserved for 'petukhi' inmates; pariah-caste members are segregated here to enforce their exclusion and to limit contact with higher-caste prisoners.", "english_term": null, "english_translation_quality": "none", "notes": "Literally 'rooster coop'; the physical embodiment of caste segregation. Segregation is enforced both by guards and by inmate social pressure; petukhi who leave their area risk violence from other castes."}
{"term": "масти", "lang": "ru", "script": "cyrl", "romanization": "masti", "category": "honor_shame_economy", "subcategory": "prison_caste_system", "concept": "prison_caste_hierarchy", "is_novel_concept": true, "register": "subculture", "crude": 20, "technical": 25, "recognition_pct_current": 70, "recognition_pct_peak": 75, "peak_year": null, "regional": null, "definition": "The prison caste ranks or 'suits' — the formal social strata of Russian prison culture, typically воры, мужики, козлы/обиженные, and петухи — each governed by strict behavioral codes and sexual taboos whose violation triggers forced caste demotion.", "english_term": null, "english_translation_quality": "none", "notes": "From масть (suit in cards; coat color in horses). The sexual dimension is definitional: a prisoner's caste status is determined partly by who they will and will not have sex with, and in what role. Crossing a caste boundary sexually results in immediate and permanent demotion."}
{"term": "кастовая система", "lang": "ru", "script": "cyrl", "romanization": "kastovaya sistema", "category": "honor_shame_economy", "subcategory": "prison_caste_system", "concept": "prison_caste_hierarchy", "is_novel_concept": true, "register": "subculture", "crude": 10, "technical": 40, "recognition_pct_current": 75, "recognition_pct_peak": 78, "peak_year": null, "regional": null, "definition": "The formalized caste system of Russian prisons (понятия), in which social position is determined largely by sexual conduct and coded prohibitions; sexual contact across caste lines triggers permanent downward reclassification.", "english_term": null, "english_translation_quality": "none", "notes": "A descriptive phrase borrowed from anthropology; used by academics, journalists, and inmates alike. Has no Western equivalent because the sexual dimension — who penetrates whom — is the primary axis of social stratification rather than wealth or violence alone."}
{"term": "чёрт", "lang": "ru", "script": "cyrl", "romanization": "chort", "category": "honor_shame_economy", "subcategory": "prison_devil_caste", "concept": "prison_devil_caste", "is_novel_concept": true, "register": "subculture", "crude": 60, "technical": 5, "recognition_pct_current": 44, "recognition_pct_peak": 50, "peak_year": null, "regional": null, "definition": "A specific prison caste label for inmates who perform menial and degrading tasks (cleaning latrines, running errands for higher castes) and are considered tainted, but who have not been sexually penetrated and thus rank above the petukhi.", "english_term": null, "english_translation_quality": "none", "notes": "The sexual distinction is crucial: чёрты have avoided forced penetration and therefore retain marginally more dignity than petukhi, though they are still below мужики. The term is everyday Russian for 'devil.' Regional usage varies; in some prison systems the category does not exist or is merged with обиженные."}
{"term": "опущен в петухи", "lang": "ru", "script": "cyrl", "romanization": "opushchen v petukhi", "category": "honor_shame_economy", "subcategory": "prison_caste_demotion", "concept": "prison_sexual_demotion", "is_novel_concept": true, "register": "subculture", "crude": 90, "technical": 5, "recognition_pct_current": 65, "recognition_pct_peak": 68, "peak_year": null, "regional": null, "definition": "Literally 'lowered into the roosters'; the formulaic phrase describing a prisoner's completed sexual demotion to the pariah caste, functioning simultaneously as a description of the assault and as a permanent identity label.", "english_term": null, "english_translation_quality": "none", "notes": "Past passive participial construction. Once this label applies, the person is barred from sharing food, utensils, or physical space with higher-caste inmates. The phrase appears in legal testimony, prison memoirs, and journalism about the Russian penal system."}
{"term": "нижний", "lang": "ru", "script": "cyrl", "romanization": "nizhny", "category": "identities_orientations", "subcategory": "sexual_role", "concept": "sexual_bottom", "is_novel_concept": false, "register": "subculture", "crude": 30, "technical": 20, "recognition_pct_current": 62, "recognition_pct_peak": 65, "peak_year": null, "regional": null, "definition": "Literally 'lower'; slang for the receptive partner in anal intercourse, used in both gay and prison contexts; in prison culture carries strong stigma because it implies low caste or forced submission.", "english_term": "bottom", "english_translation_quality": "direct", "notes": "Contrasts with верхний (upper/top). In prison culture, being the нижний is not a preference but a caste marker implying either voluntary subordination or coerced submission; the word imports none of that stigma in consensual gay usage."}
{"term": "пассив", "lang": "ru", "script": "cyrl", "romanization": "passiv", "category": "identities_orientations", "subcategory": "sexual_role", "concept": "anal_sex_receptive", "is_novel_concept": false, "register": "subculture", "crude": 40, "technical": 25, "recognition_pct_current": 70, "recognition_pct_peak": 73, "peak_year": null, "regional": null, "definition": "The receptive partner in anal intercourse; used in gay slang as a neutral role identifier and in prison slang as a stigmatizing identity label; also appears in personal ads and dating profiles.", "english_term": "passive (bottom)", "english_translation_quality": "direct", "notes": "Borrowed from Latin via technical vocabulary. In consensual gay culture, пассив is a neutral self-description; in prison culture it implies either forced penetration or caste membership in the petukhi. Contrasted with актив."}
{"term": "активный", "lang": "ru", "script": "cyrl", "romanization": "aktivny", "category": "identities_orientations", "subcategory": "sexual_role", "concept": "sexual_top", "is_novel_concept": false, "register": "subculture", "crude": 20, "technical": 25, "recognition_pct_current": 75, "recognition_pct_peak": 78, "peak_year": null, "regional": null, "definition": "The insertive and active partner in anal intercourse; in prison culture this role carries no stigma and does not lower the actor's caste status; in gay community usage it is a neutral role self-identifier.", "english_term": "top (active)", "english_translation_quality": "direct", "notes": "A key asymmetry of Russian prison sexual ethics: a higher-caste prisoner who penetrates a петух does not demote himself; only the receptive role triggers caste demotion. This asymmetry is absent in consensual gay culture, where the role is simply a preference."}
{"term": "активная роль", "lang": "ru", "script": "cyrl", "romanization": "aktivnaya rol'", "category": "identities_orientations", "subcategory": "sexual_role", "concept": "sexual_top", "is_novel_concept": false, "register": "subculture", "crude": 10, "technical": 35, "recognition_pct_current": 72, "recognition_pct_peak": 75, "peak_year": null, "regional": null, "definition": "The insertive role in sexual intercourse, especially anal; the role that is socially neutral or valorized in Russian prison culture and a neutral preference marker in consensual gay discourse.", "english_term": "active role (top role)", "english_translation_quality": "direct", "notes": "More clinical and descriptive than the bare adjective активный; used in sexology, gay-community discussions, and prison sociology. The phrase mirrors the formal language of Soviet-era sexological writing."}
{"term": "пассивная роль", "lang": "ru", "script": "cyrl", "romanization": "passivnaya rol'", "category": "identities_orientations", "subcategory": "sexual_role", "concept": "sexual_bottom", "is_novel_concept": false, "register": "subculture", "crude": 15, "technical": 35, "recognition_pct_current": 72, "recognition_pct_peak": 75, "peak_year": null, "regional": null, "definition": "The receptive role in sexual intercourse, especially anal; in prison culture, accepting this role results in permanent caste demotion; in consensual gay contexts, a neutral preference self-identifier.", "english_term": "passive role (bottom role)", "english_translation_quality": "direct", "notes": "More clinical and formal than пассив alone; found in sexological literature, gay-community educational materials, and prison sociological research alongside the subculture usage."}
{"term": "шконка", "lang": "ru", "script": "cyrl", "romanization": "shkonka", "category": "euphemisms_idioms", "subcategory": "prison_sex_site", "concept": "prison_sex_locus", "is_novel_concept": true, "register": "subculture", "crude": 15, "technical": 5, "recognition_pct_current": 65, "recognition_pct_peak": 68, "peak_year": null, "regional": null, "definition": "Prison bunk bed; the primary site of clandestine and forced sexual activity in Russian prisons, metonymically associated with the power relations and sexual coercion enacted there.", "english_term": null, "english_translation_quality": "none", "notes": "The word itself is not sexual — it denotes a prison-issue bunk — but is included in this corpus because prison sexuality discourse uses it as the metonymic site of sexual contact. The term is opaque to speakers without prison exposure; recognized primarily through crime drama and prison literature."}
{"term": "нычка", "lang": "ru", "script": "cyrl", "romanization": "nychka", "category": "euphemisms_idioms", "subcategory": "prison_sex_site", "concept": "prison_sex_locus", "is_novel_concept": true, "register": "subculture", "crude": 15, "technical": 5, "recognition_pct_current": 52, "recognition_pct_peak": 56, "peak_year": null, "regional": null, "definition": "A hidden spot or concealed stash in prison; used for hiding contraband and for conducting clandestine sexual activity away from guard observation and rival inmates.", "english_term": null, "english_translation_quality": "none", "notes": "Like шконка, not inherently sexual but functions in this corpus as a site term for covert prison sex. Diminutive form, connoting smallness and concealment. Recognized mainly through crime fiction and prison journalism."}
{"term": "аморальный", "lang": "ru", "script": "cyrl", "romanization": "amoral'ny", "category": "religious_moral_status", "subcategory": "moral_condemnation", "concept": "indecency", "is_novel_concept": false, "register": "moral_religious", "crude": 10, "technical": 20, "recognition_pct_current": 90, "recognition_pct_peak": 93, "peak_year": null, "regional": null, "definition": "Immoral; in Soviet and post-Soviet usage applied to persons or behaviors that violate sexual propriety norms — extramarital sex, homosexuality, public lewdness — and marked the subject as unfit for collective life.", "english_term": "immoral", "english_translation_quality": "approximate", "notes": "In Soviet bureaucratic language 'аморальный поступок' (immoral act) was a formal charge at workplace committees; conviction could cost Party membership, promotion, or employment. The word remains in active everyday use but has lost its institutional teeth post-1991."}
{"term": "аморалка", "lang": "ru", "script": "cyrl", "romanization": "amoralka", "category": "religious_moral_status", "subcategory": "soviet_sexual_shame", "concept": "soviet_sexual_misconduct_charge", "is_novel_concept": true, "register": "dated_slang", "crude": 20, "technical": 10, "recognition_pct_current": 62, "recognition_pct_peak": 82, "peak_year": 1975, "regional": null, "definition": "Soviet-era colloquial for a formal charge of sexual immorality leveled at a person by a work brigade, Komsomol cell, or party committee; also the collective hearing at which such a charge was adjudicated.", "english_term": null, "english_translation_quality": "none", "notes": "Unique to the Soviet institutional practice of collective moral oversight with no Western equivalent. A confirmed аморалка could result in expulsion from the Party, public shaming at a general meeting, job loss, or housing deprivation. Survives post-1991 in ironic and nostalgic usage."}
{"term": "распущенность", "lang": "ru", "script": "cyrl", "romanization": "raspushchennost'", "category": "religious_moral_status", "subcategory": "moral_condemnation", "concept": "licentiousness", "is_novel_concept": false, "register": "moral_religious", "crude": 20, "technical": 15, "recognition_pct_current": 80, "recognition_pct_peak": 88, "peak_year": null, "regional": null, "definition": "Sexual and moral dissolution; the state of being loosened from social and sexual restraint; used by Soviet ideologues to condemn Western permissiveness and by Russian conservatives to criticize contemporary sexual mores.", "english_term": "licentiousness", "english_translation_quality": "direct", "notes": "From распустить ('to let loose/unravel'); the implication is that social bonds have been allowed to slacken, releasing the person from appropriate sexual constraint. Ideologically loaded in Soviet discourse as a charge against bourgeois Western influence."}
{"term": "нескромность", "lang": "ru", "script": "cyrl", "romanization": "neskromnost'", "category": "religious_moral_status", "subcategory": "moral_condemnation", "concept": "indecency", "is_novel_concept": false, "register": "moral_religious", "crude": 10, "technical": 15, "recognition_pct_current": 78, "recognition_pct_peak": 86, "peak_year": null, "regional": null, "definition": "Immodesty; the violation of standards of sexual and social propriety through dress, speech, or behavior, particularly applied to women in Soviet-era moral discourse as evidence of compromised moral character.", "english_term": "immodesty", "english_translation_quality": "direct", "notes": "Negation of скромность (modesty); in Soviet moral discourse, нескромность in dress or sexual behavior was a strike against a woman's моральный облик that could be raised at a collective meeting. Applies to both sexual and non-sexual conduct but the sexual dimension is primary in this corpus."}
{"term": "целомудренный", "lang": "ru", "script": "cyrl", "romanization": "tselomudrennyy", "category": "religious_moral_status", "subcategory": "sexual_purity", "concept": "sexual_conservatism", "is_novel_concept": false, "register": "literary", "crude": 5, "technical": 20, "recognition_pct_current": 72, "recognition_pct_peak": 82, "peak_year": null, "regional": null, "definition": "Chaste; sexually pure in thought and action; in both Orthodox Christian theology and Soviet moral ideology, the valued state of sexual restraint before marriage, particularly for women.", "english_term": "chaste", "english_translation_quality": "approximate", "notes": "Etymology: цело (whole/intact) + мудрость (wisdom); literally 'whole-wisdom' or 'intact-wisdom,' framing chastity as cognitive and moral integrity rather than merely physical virginity. The word is more elevated and literary than its Soviet-era deployment might suggest."}
{"term": "скромность", "lang": "ru", "script": "cyrl", "romanization": "skromnost'", "category": "religious_moral_status", "subcategory": "sexual_purity", "concept": "sexual_conservatism", "is_novel_concept": false, "register": "moral_religious", "crude": 5, "technical": 15, "recognition_pct_current": 96, "recognition_pct_peak": 97, "peak_year": null, "regional": null, "definition": "Modesty; the valued quality of restraint and decorum in sexual matters, dress, and self-presentation; a central Soviet moral virtue for women that encompassed sexual unavailability, unprovocative dress, and reluctance to discuss personal sexuality.", "english_term": "modesty", "english_translation_quality": "approximate", "notes": "One of the most common and broadly positive words in Russian; in the sexual dimension, скромность means projecting sexual unavailability and discretion — the antonym of распущенность. English 'modesty' covers multiple senses; the sexual-restraint dimension is what this corpus captures."}
{"term": "моральный облик", "lang": "ru", "script": "cyrl", "romanization": "moral'nyy oblik", "category": "honor_shame_economy", "subcategory": "soviet_public_morality", "concept": "soviet_moral_public_character", "is_novel_concept": true, "register": "moral_religious", "crude": 5, "technical": 30, "recognition_pct_current": 63, "recognition_pct_peak": 87, "peak_year": 1970, "regional": null, "definition": "Literally 'moral image' or 'moral profile'; the Soviet concept of a person's publicly assessed moral standing, including sexual conduct, family stability, and collective behavior, used by party committees to evaluate fitness for promotion, Party membership, or positions of trust.", "english_term": null, "english_translation_quality": "none", "notes": "Sexual behavior was a primary component: extramarital affairs, homosexuality, or promiscuity were formal strikes against one's моральный облик. The concept presupposes a collective body with authority to evaluate and discipline private sexuality — an institutional structure without a Western equivalent."}
{"term": "разложение нравов", "lang": "ru", "script": "cyrl", "romanization": "razlozheniye nravov", "category": "religious_moral_status", "subcategory": "moral_condemnation", "concept": "debauchery", "is_novel_concept": false, "register": "literary", "crude": 15, "technical": 25, "recognition_pct_current": 66, "recognition_pct_peak": 80, "peak_year": 1972, "regional": null, "definition": "Moral decay or decomposition of customs; the Soviet and conservative Russian phrase for the decline of sexual and social morality, typically attributed to Western capitalist influence, permissiveness, or deliberate ideological subversion.", "english_term": "moral dissolution", "english_translation_quality": "approximate", "notes": "Разложение means decomposition or putrefaction, applied to нравы (customs/mores); the metaphor frames sexual permissiveness as biological rot. Standard Soviet ideological formulation used to condemn homosexuality, pornography, and rock music alike; still deployed by Russian Orthodox Church spokespeople."}
{"term": "антиобщественное поведение", "lang": "ru", "script": "cyrl", "romanization": "antiobshchestvennoye povedeniye", "category": "religious_moral_status", "subcategory": "soviet_legal_morality", "concept": "indecent_act", "is_novel_concept": false, "register": "moral_religious", "crude": 10, "technical": 40, "recognition_pct_current": 63, "recognition_pct_peak": 82, "peak_year": 1980, "regional": null, "definition": "Anti-social behavior; the Soviet legal and moral catch-all phrase applied to behaviors deemed harmful to socialist society, including homosexuality, prostitution, public drunkenness, and sexual promiscuity.", "english_term": "anti-social behavior", "english_translation_quality": "approximate", "notes": "In Soviet law, male homosexuality was classified under this category alongside hooliganism until Russia decriminalized it in 1993. The phrase remains in active conservative and legal discourse as a condemnation of sexual nonconformity; approximate match only because the English phrase lacks the Soviet legal-punitive weight."}
